{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793654408,"nonce":"jAZPCp7n8+4MtAejqlo+MUtyqMA=","title":"Plan a worker-owned OffscreenCanvas web runner for component demos","message":"Owner decision, 2026-11-02: change the planned WASM approach to OffscreenCanvas with browser agents. The website remains GIF-first with Preview | Live | Code, and starts preparing the current page's live demo as soon as the visitor arrives. The target runner places the actual WASM application, UI updates and GPU rendering in a dedicated browser worker, rather than using a worker only to compile a module for a DOM-bound renderer.\n\nThis is foundational work for website/catalog plan 5d5fc093edeffe94e65d3a1a9d8b87b62cddb41749cdde9ed336de7c7d990ad1 and the blocks plan 5ce78232a18280148736276979dbaeda0b8edd340c8eaf1ac20cacad1d2cdf07. This task records the architecture and validation work; implementation and browser-agent runs have not started.\n\nOwnership and startup\n- The browser host owns the description, GIF, tabs, canvas DOM element, focus/text-input integration, sizing and browser-only services. It calls transferControlToOffscreen before creating any context on that canvas, then transfers the OffscreenCanvas once to a dedicated worker.\n- The worker fetches/initializes WASM, loads worker-compatible resources, creates the GPU adapter/device/surface and owns the Blinc UI, reactive graph, theme, render resources and frame scheduling. The pinned wgpu26.0.1 already exposes SurfaceTarget::OffscreenCanvas; audit enabled web-sys features and adapter/backend support in the actual runner.\n- Each demo instance has one clear worker/canvas/runtime owner. Keep the existing isolated iframe host where useful; the iframe can own the DOM bridge and worker without requiring a website framework rewrite.\n- Begin startup on component-page visit while the high-resolution headless GIF plays. Signal Live Ready only after the worker reports successful asset/setup completion and a rendered frame. Selecting Live reveals the existing worker-rendered surface. Early selection retains the preview/loading presentation until it is ready.\n- Preview remains selected until the visitor chooses another tab. Once warm, suspend worker rendering while Live is inactive according to an explicit scheduling contract. Resuming, resetting, changing examples, handling device loss and navigating away must be explicit lifecycle operations.\n- A transferred canvas cannot be transferred a second time. Worker restart/recovery that requires a fresh transfer creates a new canvas, restores controlled demo inputs as appropriate and disposes the old instance.\n\nSplit the current DOM-dependent runner\n- WebApp::new currently looks up window/document/HtmlCanvasElement by ID; BlincApp's canvas creation API accepts HtmlCanvasElement. Add an OffscreenCanvas construction path and separate common frame/UI logic from DOM-specific host setup. Preserve existing desktop/mobile and supported main-thread web entry points while the new worker path is validated.\n- DOM listeners forward normalized pointer coordinates, buttons/modifiers, wheel/gesture phases and focus/keyboard events through a typed, versioned protocol. Pointer capture and synchronous browser-default handling remain at the host; worker messages have defined ordering, timestamps and demo-instance IDs. Coalesce high-rate motion/resize updates without dropping press/release, gesture boundaries or text edits.\n- Host ResizeObserver/DPR updates send logical viewport and physical pixel size. Worker size changes update the surface and layout consistently, including while the canvas is not visibly presented. Worker code reads no DOM dimensions.\n- Text input, IME/composition, selection, clipboard and browser user-activation requirements need an explicit main-thread service bridge. Forward existing editing semantics rather than duplicating CN editors in JavaScript. Reuse the shared semantic input/event model in desktop, recorder and worker paths where applicable.\n- Audit frame clocks/scheduler, asset/font/image loading, platform focus/visibility, logging/error reporting and remaining window/document accesses. The web asset loader currently calls web_sys::window; worker initialization cannot simply reuse that call. Route worker-compatible fetch/timers through WorkerGlobalScope and delegate actual DOM-only services to the host.\n- Include CSS/theme controls, prop changes and typed event callbacks in the bridge. They enter the worker's real Blinc stylesheet and Signal/Computed/Effect bindings, preserving the existing CSS-first, prop-binding contracts and documented restyling surface.\n- Workers own their own WASM/runtime resources. This design does not itself call for shared-memory WASM threading or a new reactive engine.\n\nLifecycle and capability behavior\n- Protocol covers boot/progress/first-frame/ready/error, input, size/DPR, visibility, theme/CSS/props, pause/resume, reset and dispose. Request/instance IDs prevent stale worker completion from updating a newer demo page.\n- Keep the page and GIF usable throughout initialization/failure. Detect actual worker surface/device support; test the selected WebGPU/WebGL2 path instead of inferring success from an API name. Document the resulting support matrix.\n- Use explicit pause/teardown and stop acknowledgements where appropriate; termination is the final disposal path. Verify timers, input hooks, GPU contexts/resources and subscriptions do not accumulate through tab changes or navigation.\n- Browser-agent tests exercise the worker implementation on dedicated test pages/CI browser sessions. Main-thread fallback, where retained, is a separately reported compatibility path; a successful main-thread test must not certify the worker runner.\n\nImplementation sequence\n1. A focused worker boot/OffscreenCanvas surface spike renders a small existing Blinc demo, reports first frame and supports resize/disposal. Confirm installed wgpu/web-sys APIs and browser capability before moving the full runner.\n2. Separate shared frame execution from DOM/platform services; add the worker-safe constructor, scheduling and asset path. Start with a button/progress demo and validate the protocol and error handling.\n3. Bridge pointer, wheel, keyboard/focus and editor/IME/clipboard services; exercise CN input/select/dialog/toast and theme/CSS controls. Reuse existing recorder scenarios for equivalent behavior.\n4. Integrate the GIF-first catalog host, startup readiness, Preview/Live/Code switching, example reset and repeated navigation. Then expand to charts and blocks.\n\nBrowser-agent validation\n- Verify startup occurs on page visit while GIF Preview remains responsive; first-frame and ready signals reflect worker rendering.\n- Exercise actual canvas behavior with pointer, keyboard, focus, scrolling/gestures, editing and popup/overlay dismissal; check readiness/event messages and rendered screenshots. Clicking DOM tab buttons alone is not sufficient coverage of a canvas component.\n- Verify resize, DPR, narrow layout, switching tabs during startup, pause/resume, reset, worker failure/device loss recovery and navigation cancellation/cleanup.\n- Compare representative worker-rendered screenshots and interactions with headless/native fixtures and supported main-thread web output. Record backend/browser results and inspect images rather than assuming cross-backend pixel identity.\n- Measure main-thread responsiveness, time to first frame/interaction, hidden/idle work and resource retention. Demonstrate that WASM startup/render work is actually in the worker; no unmeasured performance promise.\n- Validation stays scoped to touched runner/demo/browser cases, Cargo jobs2, with no automation of the owner's desktop. No browser-agent session, build or production source change was launched for this planning update.\n\nReferences and code anchors\nhttps://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HTMLCanvasElement/transferControlToOffscreen\nhttps://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/OffscreenCanvas\nhttps://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WorkerNavigator/gpu\nLocal: crates/blinc_app/src/{web,app}.rs; extensions/blinc_platform_web/src/{window,assets}.rs; tools/build-web-examples/src/main.rs. Pinned wgpu26.0.1 source api/surface.rs includes the OffscreenCanvas surface target.","files":null}]}